    "result_text": "I added the three password reset helpers to `CTFd/utils/security/email.py`. I haven't run any tests or the app.\n\n- **`generate_reset_password_token(addr, timeout=1800)`** makes a random 256-bit token, stores the email in the cache for 30 minutes, and returns the token.\n- **`verify_reset_password_token(nonce)`** returns the email for the token. It raises `UserResetPasswordTokenInvalidException` if the token is unknown or expired.\n- **`remove_reset_password_token(nonce)`** deletes the token, so it can't be used again.\n\nThey follow the catalog pattern `security.email_confirm_token@v1`, with `# pcat:` markers kept above each function. The cache key prefix is `reset_password_`, so reset tokens can't be confused with email-confirmation tokens. `auth.py` and `utils/initialization/__init__.py` already imported these names, so this should also fix that missing code.\n\n**A human needs to confirm one approval.** `check_code` flagged two high-severity deviations on `verify_reset_password_token` (PC100 and PC102). The pattern raises `UserConfirmTokenInvalidException`, and I raise the reset-specific exception instead. The callers in `auth.py` and `initialization/__init__.py` catch the reset exception, so the pattern's exception would break their handling. The invalid-token check itself is unchanged. I added two `# pcat-allow` lines (hash `514686d7`) above that function. They don't count until a human confirms them in the pull request. The other flags were low severity (the changed cache key prefix) and need no action."
  },
